Here are the public test cases for feature1_required_positional — study the exact value rendering, especially the quoted strings and trailing newline:

{
  "description": "An action declaring only required positional parameters has each one filled, in order, from the request bag by matching parameter name; an action declaring no parameters receives no injected arguments.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          },
          {
            "name": "b",
            "kind": "required"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=b kind=required source=injected value=\"2\"\n"
    }
  ]
}

Note the semantics while you read it: extra bag entries (like "b" when not declared) are simply ignored, and output lines follow the signature's declared order. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature2_missing_required — note that on failure the error block replaces the entire output, no partial binding lines are emitted:

{
  "description": "When a required parameter (positional or by-name) is absent from the request bag, injection fails with a normalized missing-required error listing the absent parameter names.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "required"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "error=missing_required\nmissing=x\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required_keyword"
          },
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "required_keyword"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "error=missing_required\nmissing=x\n"
    }
  ]
}

While you read it: the error output is exactly two lines — `error=missing_required` then `missing=<names>` — and this applies identically to required positional and required_keyword, as case 2 demonstrates. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature3_optional_positional — note that even a declared-default value renders as a quoted string with source=default:

{
  "description": "A single optional positional parameter takes the bag value when its name is present, otherwise it keeps its declared default and is omitted from injection.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "a"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=optional source=injected value=\"1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=x kind=optional source=default value=\"x\"\n"
    }
  ]
}

Read it carefully: case 2 is the trailing-absent-optional scenario — with nothing supplied after it, it keeps its declared default (source=default). That's the contrast point against the <nil> placeholder cases you'll see later. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature4_required_then_optional:

{
  "description": "An action with a leading required parameter followed by a trailing optional one: the required parameter is filled from the bag, and the optional one takes its bag value when present or its declared default when absent.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          },
          {
            "name": "b",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "b"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=b kind=optional source=injected value=\"2\"\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          },
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=x kind=optional source=default value=\"x\"\n"
    }
  ]
}

While you read it: output lines still follow the signature's declared order — required first, optional after — regardless of bag iteration. The required parameter always gets its bag value, no exceptions. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature5_consecutive_optionals — this is the file where the nil-placeholder rule first shows its teeth:

{
  "description": "Two consecutive optional positional parameters are each resolved against the bag independently; a present name is injected (overriding the default) while an absent name keeps its declared default.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          },
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "a"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=optional source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=x kind=optional source=injected value=<nil>\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "a"
          },
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=optional source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=x kind=optional source=default value=\"x\"\n"
    }
  ]
}

Two things to stare at in case 1: (1) declared order is [x, a] but output is [a, x] — bindings print sorted alphabetically by parameter name, period. (2) x is absent yet does NOT keep its default — because a supplied parameter sits after it, x is positionally forced into an explicit `value=<nil>` with `source=injected`. Also note the description text says the two are 'resolved against the bag independently' — the raw case description is looser than the actual expected output, so trust the expected_output bytes, not the prose. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature6_optional_before_required — the companion to feature5, now with a required param anchoring the tail:

{
  "description": "When optional positional parameters precede a trailing required parameter, the leading optionals must be filled positionally so the required one keeps its value; an absent leading optional that sits before a supplied parameter is injected as an explicit empty placeholder rather than taking its default.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          },
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=x kind=optional source=default value=\"x\"\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "x"
          },
          {
            "name": "b",
            "kind": "optional",
            "default": "b"
          },
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=b kind=optional source=injected value=\"2\"\narg=x kind=optional source=injected value=<nil>\n"
    }
  ]
}

Dissect case 2 precisely: declared order [x, b, a]; a is required and always takes its bag value; b is present so injected; x is absent but sits before supplied parameters (b, a), so it is positionally forced to `value=<nil>` with `source=injected` — the declared default "x" is discarded. And again the output lines are [a, b, x]: sorted alphabetically by name, not declared order. Compare against case 1, where nothing is supplied after x, so x keeps its default. Next file when you're ready — ask by name.

Here are the public test cases for feature7_keyword_arguments:

{
  "description": "By-name keyword parameters are bound from the bag by matching name; a present optional keyword is injected, an absent optional keyword keeps its declared default, and a present required keyword is injected.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "optional_keyword",
            "default": null
          },
          {
            "name": "x",
            "kind": "optional_keyword",
            "default": "x"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=optional_keyword source=injected value=\"1\"\narg=x kind=optional_keyword source=default value=\"x\"\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "signature": [
          {
            "name": "a",
            "kind": "required_keyword"
          }
        ],
        "params": {
          "a": "1",
          "b": "2"
        }
      },
      "expected_output": "arg=a kind=required_keyword source=injected value=\"1\"\n"
    }
  ]
}

Three details to lock in: (1) case 1 declared order is [a, x] and output happens to match — but don't read that as declared order; the alphabetical sorting rule still governs, it just coincides here. (2) Note the difference from the positional world: an absent optional_keyword (x) keeps its declared default even though another keyword param was supplied — no <nil> forcing for keywords, ever. That's because keywords are matched purely by name and never participate in positional slot-filling. (3) A default may be null in the signature — if you ever need to know how a null default renders, ask precisely. That's all seven files handed over. Now go build, and come back when you have a design to review.
